{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793453995,"nonce":"aNPYzjVWWpUID0zW4nAtiuELiuI=","title":"A thread blocked reading a child's pipe stalls the collector for the whole program","message":"Reported by the RayDE session. sys.io.Process stdout/stderr reads, stdin writes and the blocking exit wait called into std without hl_blocking, so a thread parked on an idle child never reached a safepoint. Upstream HashLink brackets these calls with hl_blocking.\n\nReproducer: a Haxe program starts `sleep 300` with sys.io.Process, reads its stdout in a Thread, and allocates in a loop on the main thread. Before the fix: \"[gc] gave up stopping the world after ~2000ms; 1 of 10 mutators never reached a safepoint\" and the program does not finish within 60 s in hybrid or jit. After: finishes in about 3 s. RayDE's SurfacePump parks two such readers, so an idle child stalled the viewer.\n\nFix brackets hlp_process_stdout_read, stderr_read, stdin_write and the blocking exit wait (including their mutex acquisition) with blocking_io. Regression test: crates/ash_cli/tests/process_pipe_gc.rs, which fails without the change.\n\nNot covered: --mode interp still hangs on this program, with and without the fix, because interpreted threads share one OS thread (see the fiber blocking-IO deadlock). Other blocking primitives (condition waits, sleeps) were not audited.","files":null}]}